## Context
All number formatting lives in `tl-model::units`, so tables, graphs, tooltips, copy texts and the tray print the same strings (canonical table (d), R-013, R-014, R-015, PROC-003, PERF-025). Formatters append to a caller-provided `String` and never allocate when the buffer has room (ADR-003). They print only valid values: missing values use validity glyphs and words in the UI, never a zero. This bead covers percent, sizes and rates; E03-013 covers counts, times, frequencies, temperatures, power, caches, addresses and response time. Sources: RECONCILIATION table (d), R-013, R-014, R-015, R-094, R-081 (decimal point "." in every language), PROC-003, PERF-025, ADR-003 "Units and formatting".

## Deliverable
`crates/tl-model/src/units.rs` (first half). Every function appends to `out` (it does not clear it) using `core::fmt::Write`; none returns an error (writing to `String` cannot fail; `unwrap` is not used, the `fmt::Result` is ignored with `let _ =`). Decimal point is always "."; no locale forms.

Shared rule, "measured zero": a value that is exactly 0, or that rounds to 0 at the printed precision, prints the zero form of its row. Negative or NaN input prints the zero form too (rates and sizes are never negative; NaN is a caller bug and must not panic).

